WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.300
Welcome to Ear Biscuits, the podcast where

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.780
two lifelong friends talk
about life for a long time.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:10.740
I'm Link.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:11.573
And I'm Rhett.

00:00:11.573 --> 00:00:13.530
This week at the Round
Table of Dim Lighting,

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:17.820
we are responding to your
questions and disputes

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.477
that you brought to us when we asked you,

00:00:19.477 --> 00:00:23.250
"Are you beefing with a friend,
family member, or partner?

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.530
Let us settle your
dispute once and for all.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:29.100
Serious, funny, or bizarre,
we're here to help."

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:29.952
That's us.

00:00:29.952 --> 00:00:30.785
Give us a call at

00:00:30.785 --> 00:00:33.990
1-88-EARPOD1.
EARPOD1.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.030
So we're gonna do that with our many years

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:39.690
of settling disputes, both with
each other and with others.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.670
We're somewhat experts.
We're peacemakers.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.070
We're peacemakers, but
we're problem solvers.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:44.903
Problem solvers.

00:00:44.903 --> 00:00:45.736
And today,
Engineers.

00:00:45.736 --> 00:00:48.570
it's not just about making
peace, it's about being right.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:49.620
Yeah, right.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.030
We're gonna tell you who definitively

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.830
in your dispute is right.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.140
Unless we disagree, which
is gonna get awkward.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.380
But before we get into that,
I have a little update.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.110
I want to give you a little
something I've been sitting on

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.360
since I arrived here this morning.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.193
Oh.

00:01:04.193 --> 00:01:06.240
As you know, I played in my first ever

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.925
celebrity golf tournament yesterday.

00:01:08.925 --> 00:01:10.297
I did know that.
Ha-ha, yes I did.

00:01:10.297 --> 00:01:12.570
Oh, yeah, so you gotta
play with a celebrity?

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:13.403
Yeah, I did.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.260
Yeah, it was the Comedy Gives Back,

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.590
2nd Annual Comedy Gives Back
Celebrity Golf Tournament.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.960
And it was funny because I just,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.730
it's something I've always wanted to do.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:27.563
Like I see-

00:01:27.563 --> 00:01:28.540
You want to give back to comedy?

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:29.373
Well, yeah, that.

00:01:29.373 --> 00:01:32.670
I see the, like the
celebrity Pro-ams and stuff,

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.950
and I like golf and I,
at least in some circles,

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.390
am considered a celebrity.

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.090
So I was like, just, you
know, little bucket list item.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.133
Let's play in one and see what happens.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:45.270
So, because I guess in some
senses, I am a comedian,

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:50.270
I was included in this group
of about 25 stand-up comedians.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.500
Oh.
Including

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.561
the one and only Bill Burr.

00:01:54.561 --> 00:01:56.880
What a standout stand-up comedian.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.190
Yeah, so, like legit stand-up comedians.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:00.132
Oh, yeah.

00:02:00.132 --> 00:02:03.000
Like, respected and
established stand-up comedians

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.980
who are so good at stand-up
comedy that they get parts

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.380
in The Mandalorian, like,
those kinds of people, right?

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.370
Yep.

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.283
He was the most well-known one there.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.550
In fact, he may have been the only one who

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.320
they auctioned off playing with him,

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.500
but they didn't auction off
playing with anybody else.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.350
So, you were assigned to like a foursome?

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.000
Yeah, a foursome.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.130
That ended up being a
twosome and then a threesome,

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.220
and then the fourth guy didn't
show up 'cause he got COVID.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.920
Long story, but it was, what
they ended up doing for me,

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.223
I guess because I was the
oddball, the internet guy,

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.510
is as opposed to putting me
with a group of four friends

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.330
or like, a group of four
people from a company,

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:45.480
there were the reps for the
brands that were doing things.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.090
So, like the Liquid Death
guy, 'cause Liquid Death was

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:49.290
supplying the water for this.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:50.670
Just like they supplied for MythiCon.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:51.570
Big fan.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:54.150
The guy who was supplying
the shirts for everybody.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:54.983
And then-

00:02:54.983 --> 00:02:55.883
So, you were the brand

00:02:55.883 --> 00:02:57.732
liaison comedian.
And then the Porsche guy.

00:02:57.732 --> 00:02:59.580
There was a hole where
if you got a hole in one,

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.640
you won a Porsche Macan or whatever you-

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.050
Oh.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.573
Is it Mecan or Pecan? Mecahn?

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.484
That guy, and then there was
another guy who got COVID

00:03:10.484 --> 00:03:11.317
and didn't show up.

00:03:11.317 --> 00:03:14.520
So anyway, we did not win.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:15.840
It was fun, yes.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.540
But the thing that I was
actually kind of nervous about

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.370
is that they said that
there was going to be

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:24.120
a joke-telling contest at night, at dinner

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.030
in front of all these
comedians and all the teams.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:30.270
And that the comedian from
the team is responsible

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.580
for coming up with a joke
and delivering the joke.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:37.080
Joke-telling contest, even
that as a phrase seems, like,

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.520
antiquated and strange.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.610
Well, that's a big part of this.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:45.660
It was a joke, like a
joke-joke-telling contest.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:48.090
Not a routine, not a stand-up routine.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:49.650
Not a story that happened to you.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.140
It was like, you tell a joke joke.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.750
Like, a joke with a
set-up and a punchline.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:58.170
Which interestingly, in
that comedy community,

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:00.870
you know, especially these
guys who are of this age,

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:02.190
middle-aged or whatever,

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.410
you know, stand-up
comedy in the '80s, '90s,

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.720
They like a good joke.
there was a lot of jokes.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.190
There's kind of a tradition of jokes,

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:09.750
and so they kind of respect jokes.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:13.057
Especially, well, and
here's another thing that

00:04:13.057 --> 00:04:15.540
you would not be surprised to know.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:19.110
I would have dropped out
upon finding this out.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:23.220
Comedians, they've kind of ignored

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.500
a lot of things that have
changed in society and they still

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.540
just kind of say whatever
the hell they wanna say.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:28.373
Yeah.

00:04:28.373 --> 00:04:29.910
In one sense I sort of respect that,

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:32.640
there's a place where
that's happening in comedy.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:33.473
That's not your place.

00:04:33.473 --> 00:04:36.213
That's not my style, but I respect it.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.757
And so I knew, I was like,
because they were like,

00:04:39.757 --> 00:04:41.670
"Anything goes."

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.100
So I was like, these are
gonna, it's gonna be so dirty.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.540
People are gonna say inappropriate things,

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.730
people are gonna say
politically incorrect things.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:49.280
None of this is being recorded.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:51.500
But I was like,
Supposedly.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:56.070
I have to go, I gotta do
something dirty, right?

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:57.303
And I was also thinking,

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:01.890
I need to do something original,
I'm gonna write my joke.

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:04.530
I'm not gonna go on
Reddit and find a joke.

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:05.670
You can't do that.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.470
Even though that's what a lot of them did.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:10.080
A lot of them were like, "This
is my uncle's favorite joke."

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.940
Or, "This is a joke my dad told me

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:13.830
multiple times growing up."

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.530
That's different than
going on Reddit though.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:19.290
If you're related to the
person who told you the joke.

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:21.240
The guy hosting it was Ben Bailey.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:24.360
You may remember him as the
guy who hosted Cash Cab.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:25.193
Oh, yeah.

00:05:25.193 --> 00:05:26.610
For 14 seasons.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.130
So, he's a stand-up, so
he's MCing the thing.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:30.453
Yeah.

00:05:30.453 --> 00:05:31.422
Kind of a dry wit.

00:05:31.422 --> 00:05:34.261
Other comedians who I'm competing against.

00:05:34.261 --> 00:05:38.430
I will say Bill Burr was not
feeling great, so he didn't

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.950
stay for the joke contest,
which took some pressure off.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:42.270
Okay, good.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.830
But Randy-
Did you meet Bill?

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:45.240
I did meet Bill.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:47.400
I met him before we started playing.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.923
Told him I was representing
YouTube comedy.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:52.740
What was his reaction to that?

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:55.440
He was like, "Ah, but Bo Burnham, ah,"

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:58.964
Oh, Bo Burnham.
"I mean, that dude's crazy!"

00:05:58.964 --> 00:06:01.680
Okay, okay.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.213
So that made me feel good.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:05.580
He was very nice.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.370
And I met him at the same
time I met Greg Fitzsimmons,

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:11.220
who you would recognize if I
showed you a picture of him.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:13.560
You know, this guy, he's been like,

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:15.450
you've seen him on The
Tonight Show, whatever,

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:17.040
and he's been around forever.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:18.630
So Greg was in it,

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:21.690
And then Randy Sklar of
Randy and Jason Sklar,

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.580
Sklar Brothers was in the joke.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.520
I went right after
Randy as matter of fact.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:30.510
So, I'm gonna deliver my joke for you.

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:33.093
Deliver your joke for all of us.

00:06:33.978 --> 00:06:38.978
I'm gonna get up there and
do exactly what I did, right?

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.250
I grabbed the microphone.
Oh, you gonna stand up?

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:44.088
No, I'm gonna stay seated
because the microphone's here.

00:06:44.088 --> 00:06:45.990
And I'm gonna do word
for word what I said.

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:47.880
Sit-down comedy.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.200
And people had done a lot of, like,

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.240
classic set-up/punchline jokes,

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.490
just to let you know
the world that we're in.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:59.370
Ben Bailey says, "Rhett
McLaughlin, everybody."

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:00.630
Smattering of applause.

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:01.880
Smattering of applause.

00:07:03.517 --> 00:07:07.317
"Disclaimer: I am an internet comedian."

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:11.430
Smattering of laughter.
Okay.

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:14.180
Which means, I don't know
if they're laughing or not.

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:16.860
A little bit more laughter.

00:07:17.887 --> 00:07:19.023
"My joke is short.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:24.057
Why did my ex-wife cross the road?"

00:07:25.987 --> 00:07:26.820
"Why?"

00:07:28.177 --> 00:07:30.857
"I don't know, I don't keep
up with that fuckin' bitch."

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:34.290
Oh, wow, look at you.

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.363
Using the F-bomb and the B-bomb.

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:38.543
That was good.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:43.503
Jenna and Brian laughed at it.

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:46.590
So, and I'll tell ya...

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:49.110
I'm just over here shocked, Rhett.

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:52.740
I'm gonna, I am not a joke writer, okay?

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:54.450
So, you made that up?

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:56.100
I made that up.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:59.160
Yeah, but you don't have an ex-wife.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:00.060
Yeah, yeah.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.230
So, you also made up an ex-wife.

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:02.370
Yeah, right.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:03.450
Wow.
It was a joke.

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:04.830
The liberties that you're taking.

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:06.813
And Ben Bailey said,

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:11.220
I wouldn't say it killed, but it did well.

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:14.010
It was very respectable,
it was like, I would say,

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:15.645
I didn't make it to the top three.

00:08:15.645 --> 00:08:17.520
Why did my ex-wife cross the road?

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:18.770
And then the answer is?

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.060
Well, first you said "why?"

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:23.667
Did you have to lean back from
your own mic and say "why?"

00:08:23.667 --> 00:08:24.990
No, I was imitating the crowd.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:27.120
Or did somebody do that?

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.850
When you're telling jokes
to a crowd of comedians,

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:31.260
They know.
they say "why?"

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:32.093
Oh, okay.

00:08:32.093 --> 00:08:34.260
You know, they give you the other thing.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:37.170
And they're not gonna jump
on it and guess the answer

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:39.240
like some person at a comedy club.

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:40.073
Like, they're comedians.

00:08:40.073 --> 00:08:42.030
They're like, "This guy's telling a joke."

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:43.260
So it's a great crowd for that.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.450
I don't know, I don't keep
up with that fucking bitch.

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:46.477
Yeah, yeah.

00:08:46.477 --> 00:08:49.230
"My joke is short", tell
me about that part of it.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:50.693
Like, why did you feel like
you needed to say that?

00:08:50.693 --> 00:08:52.530
Because people had been saying that

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:54.540
if they had a short one.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:56.712
People would be like, "This
is a long one, settle in."

00:08:56.712 --> 00:08:57.810
Some people are like,
"This is a short one."

00:08:57.810 --> 00:08:59.070
I feel like the...

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:00.240
I mean, that may have been a mistake.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:03.748
I feel like if you're open
to constructive feedback.

00:09:03.748 --> 00:09:07.380
Oh, I am, but I'm not gonna
get into joke writing,

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:08.630
just so you, don't worry.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:11.520
You made an excellent choice in having

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:14.790
such a staccato joke, but I
don't think you should have

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:17.610
said "my joke is short"
because that was kind of,

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:22.020
the punch of it was just
how "bam" and it's over.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:25.710
Because the shortness of
it is part of the surprise.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:27.900
Well, but once you say, "why did so and so

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:29.177
cross the road?", you
know it's a short joke,

00:09:29.177 --> 00:09:32.130
'cause that's a classic
structure of a short joke, so.

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:33.480
Well, that's another reason
you shouldn't have said it.

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:34.624
That's a better reason though.

00:09:34.624 --> 00:09:37.170
I don't think that I cut the
joke off, but it was more

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:39.090
that I was just kinda like
feeding off of the things

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:40.170
that people had been saying.

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:42.660
I will say-
What would I have done?

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:43.789
Well, let me tell you right now.

00:09:43.789 --> 00:09:47.760
I don't know what I would've done.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:49.467
I was definitely nervous because

00:09:49.467 --> 00:09:52.360
I was just trying to save
face and say something.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:54.360
Well, you self-deprecated.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:56.640
Say something, just do
something funny and unexpected.

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:58.950
And I would say that-
You lowered the bar,

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:00.120
you made a joke about it.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:01.290
Yeah, right.

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:02.580
Then you said something
you shouldn't have said,

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:03.983
then you said a really good joke.

00:10:03.983 --> 00:10:05.070
Oh, you like it.

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:06.780
So I see that in the vein of like-

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:08.730
I'm offended.
Like, Norm Macdonald.

00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:09.930
I don't know how familiar you are with,

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:12.960
Norm Macdonald when he
was having his show,

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:16.560
his internet show, there
was this joke portion.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:19.440
And it was, every joke
that Norm Macdonald told

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:23.313
was always a misdirect,
it was like, in that way.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:25.860
So that's not based on any joke,

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:30.060
but it's based on the structure
of a Norm Macdonald joke,

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:31.680
which is like, I'm not
gonna give you the thing

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:34.260
that you thought I was going to give you,

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:37.650
I'm gonna give you, it's a
joke about the joke, kinda.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:39.840
How'd you come up with that joke?

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:41.280
It was not easy.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:43.530
Because I came up with
like four other jokes.

